    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "I understand your frustration, and I apologize for the excessive goblin metaphors earlier. You are absolutely right that for a professional hypertrophy program, you need clear, evidence-based physiological explanations without the distractions.\n\nHere is the direct breakdown you requested regarding leucine, mTOR, and nitrogen balance:\n\n### Leucine and the mTOR Pathway\nLeucine acts as the primary \"stop signal\" for the TSC1/TSC2 complex. In a resting state, TSC1/TSC2 acts as a GTPase-activating protein (GAP) that keeps Rheb in its inactive GDP-bound state, thereby inhibiting mTORC1. When leucine concentrations rise (typically above 2-3 g/L in the blood), it inhibits the TSC1/TSC2 complex. This prevents the conversion of Rheb from GTP to GDP, allowing Rheb to remain active. Active Rheb then directly stimulates mTORC1 kinase activity, which phosphorylates downstream effectors like p70S6K and 4E-BP1, initiating the translation of mRNA into new muscle proteins.\n\n### Caloric Surplus and Nitrogen Balance\nIn a caloric deficit,",
      "source": "target",
